Below are the integrity rules for support ticket records and one batch of 10 records to be checked against them. The batch breaks one of the rules at most, and then at a single spot. Only the listed rules are checked and each is read literally: data that looks odd but is covered by no rule does not count.

Fields per record: ticket_id (text), manager (text), team (text), reopens (text), opened_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), contact_box (text), case_no (text), remote_min (whole number), billed_min (whole number), contact_ext (text), closed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), account (text), escalated (text), product (text), queue_pos (whole number), onsite_min (whole number).

Rules:
R1. No record has a `closed_on` that lies before its `opened_on`; the two may coincide.
R2. A `team` that is not a key of the reference list `teams` is not allowed.
R3. Each `product` has to appear in the reference list `products` with the status "current"; an entry with any other status does not count.
R4. `queue_pos` rises by exactly 1 from each record to the next one in the batch; the first record may carry any number.
R5. Within the batch, all values of `ticket_id` differ from one another.
R6. When `reopens` is filled in, it must be the `ticket_id` of a record that stands earlier in the batch.
R7. The pair (`case_no`, `account`) may occur only once in the batch; either field on its own may repeat.
R8. Of the two fields `contact_box` and `contact_ext`, one is filled in and the other is empty, in every record.

Reference list `teams` with 8 keys: TM-10, TM-14, TM-20, TM-30, TM-41, TM-59, TM-62, TM-98

Reference list `products` with 7 entries (key: status): PR-210: current; PR-276: current; PR-352: draft; PR-569: current; PR-596: draft; PR-857: current; PR-891: current

Statuses in a reference list: a rule that only asks for a key of the list accepts every key the list holds, whatever its status; only a rule that names a status requires it.

