16 sensor reading records were delivered as one batch and are validated against the numbered rules that follow. The batch breaks one of the rules at most, and then at a single spot. Only the listed rules are checked and each is read literally: data that looks odd but is covered by no rule does not count.

Fields per record: reading_id (text), humidity (whole number), taken_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), probe (text), temp_c (whole number), alarm_code (text), script_id (text), gateway (text), alarm (text), frame_no (whole number), mode (text), uploaded_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), zone (text), cycle (text), operator (text).

Rules:
R1. `probe` and `cycle` together identify a record: two records with the same `probe` must differ in `cycle`.
R2. The lowest allowed `humidity` is 20, the highest is 80.
R3. For a record with `zone` = "chilled", `temp_c` lies between 2 and 10; for a record with `zone` = "ambient", between 7 and 38 (limits included in both cases).
R4. Accepted values of `mode`: "manual", "test", "calibration", "idle"; nothing else.
R5. `taken_on` is never after `uploaded_on`; both on the same day is acceptable.
R6. `script_id` and `operator` exclude each other, and one of them is always filled in.
R7. Each `gateway` has to appear in the reference list `gateways` with the status "active"; an entry with any other status does not count.

Reference list `gateways` with 9 entries, one per line (key: status):
GW-13: suspended
GW-20: suspended
GW-24: active
GW-44: active
GW-51: active
GW-68: suspended
GW-73: active
GW-81: inactive
GW-85: active

The status written beside a key counts only for a rule that names a status. Every other rule that refers to the list accepts all of its keys, whatever their status.

