Below are the integrity rules for library loan records and one batch of 16 records to be checked against them. Either every rule holds or exactly one is broken, at one spot. Nothing is a violation unless a listed rule, taken literally, forbids it.

Fields per record: loan_id (text), borrowed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), slip_no (whole number), member (text), desk (text), due_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), reserve_code (text), kiosk (text), condition_out (text), reserved (text), item_type (text), renewal_of (text), deposit (whole number), card (text), condition_in (text), copy (text).

Integrity rules:
R1. `borrowed_on` lies strictly before `due_on`: the same day breaks the rule.
R2. `condition_out` must be one of the following: "new", "good", "repaired".
R3. The reference list `members` must contain every value of `member`.
R4. The pair (`copy`, `card`) may occur only once in the batch; either field on its own may repeat.
R5. Of the two fields `desk` and `kiosk`, one is filled in and the other is empty, in every record.
R6. For a record with `item_type` = "media", `deposit` lies between 2 and 9; for a record with `item_type` = "book", between 6 and 30 (limits included in both cases).
R7. A record whose `reserved` is "yes" needs a filled-in `reserve_code`; for other records `reserve_code` is optional.
R8. When `renewal_of` is filled in, it must be the `loan_id` of a record that stands earlier in the batch.

