A loader validates parcel records batch by batch; the rules it applies and the next batch (24 records) are shown below. At most one rule is broken, and only in one place. A rule asks for exactly what its sentence says; whatever no listed rule covers is not a violation, however odd it looks.

Each record has these fields: parcel_id (text), gross_weight (whole number), route (text), manifest (text), scan_no (whole number), door_code (text), delivered_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), depot (text), length_cm (whole number), bag (text), piece_weight (whole number), locker_id (text), pieces (whole number), sent_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), service (text).

Rules:
R1. Of the two fields `locker_id` and `door_code`, one is filled in and the other is empty, in every record.
R2. For a record with `service` = "letter", `length_cm` lies between 12 and 40; for a record with `service` = "freight", between 30 and 200 (limits included in both cases).
R3. The pair (`manifest`, `bag`) may occur only once in the batch; either field on its own may repeat.
R4. Each `depot` has to be found among the keys of the reference list `depots`.
R5. `sent_on` lies strictly before `delivered_on`: the same day breaks the rule.
R6. A `route` is valid only if the reference list `routes` holds it and marks it "active".
R7. Within the batch, all values of `parcel_id` differ from one another.

Reference list `depots` with 15 keys: DP-195, DP-314, DP-349, DP-379, DP-388, DP-397, DP-464, DP-483, DP-507, DP-568, DP-692, DP-701, DP-898, DP-938, DP-983

