A loader validates course enrolment records batch by batch; the rules it applies and the next batch (24 records) are shown below. The batch breaks one of the rules at most, and then at a single spot. Only the listed rules are checked and each is read literally: data that looks odd but is covered by no rule does not count.

Each record has these fields: enrol_id (text), materials (whole number), transfer_of (text), lab_slot (text), credits (whole number), course (text), mode (text), needs_lab (text), student (text), starts_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), tuition (whole number), weekly_hours (whole number), amount_due (whole number), module (text), grant_id (text), ends_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), invoice_ref (text).

Integrity rules:
R1. Each `enrol_id` appears at most once in the batch; a repeated `enrol_id` breaks this rule.
R2. `starts_on` precedes `ends_on`, and the two never coincide.
R3. Whenever `needs_lab` is "yes", `lab_slot` must be filled in.
R4. `student` and `module` together identify a record: two records with the same `student` must differ in `module`.
R5. The reference list `courses` must contain every value of `course`.
R6. If `mode` is "full_time", `weekly_hours` is at least 16 and at most 38; if `mode` is "part_time", at least 3 and at most 18.
R7. In every record, `tuition` and `materials` add up to exactly `amount_due`.
R8. `credits` stays within 3 to 8, limits included.
R9. `transfer_of` may be empty; otherwise it holds a `enrol_id` found in one of the records above it.

