16 support ticket records were delivered as one batch and are validated against the numbered rules that follow. At most one rule is broken, and only in one place. A rule asks for exactly what its sentence says; whatever no listed rule covers is not a violation, however odd it looks.

Record fields: ticket_id (text), team (text), manager (text), closed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), product (text), account (text), case_no (text), reopens (text), escalated (text), queue_pos (whole number), plan (text), response_hours (whole number), opened_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), contact_ext (text), contact_box (text).

Rules:
R1. Of the two fields `contact_ext` and `contact_box`, one is filled in and the other is empty, in every record.
R2. When `reopens` is filled in, it must be the `ticket_id` of a record that stands earlier in the batch.
R3. `queue_pos` rises by exactly 1 from each record to the next one in the batch; the first record may carry any number.
R4. If `escalated` equals "yes", an empty `manager` is not allowed.
R5. Every value of `product` must be an entry of the reference list `products` that is marked "current".
R6. No record has a `closed_on` on or before its `opened_on`.
R7. `ticket_id` is the record key: a value may occur only once in the batch.
R8. A `team` that is not a key of the reference list `teams` is not allowed.
