A loader validates library loan records batch by batch; the rules it applies and the next batch (24 records) are shown below. Either every rule holds or exactly one is broken, at one spot. Nothing is a violation unless a listed rule, taken literally, forbids it.

Each record has these fields: loan_id (text), slip_no (whole number), desk (text), deposit (whole number), renewal_of (text), reserve_code (text), renewals (whole number), days_late (whole number), member (text), due_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), reserved (text), item_type (text), borrowed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), fine (whole number), kiosk (text), daily_fee (whole number).

Rules to check:
R1. The values of `slip_no` are consecutive in batch order: no number is skipped or repeated, wherever the count starts.
R2. If `item_type` is "book", `deposit` is at least 6 and at most 30; if `item_type` is "media", at least 2 and at most 9.
R3. Each `member` has to be found among the keys of the reference list `members`.
R4. The stored `fine` has to match `days_late` x `daily_fee`.
R5. `renewals` is never smaller than 0 and never larger than 4.
R6. Of the two fields `kiosk` and `desk`, one is filled in and the other is empty, in every record.
R7. A filled-in `renewal_of` names the `loan_id` of another record of this batch, and that record comes before the one that names it.
R8. Whenever `reserved` is "yes", `reserve_code` must be filled in.
R9. No record has a `due_on` that lies before its `borrowed_on`; the two may coincide.

Reference list `members` with 12 keys: MB-2476, MB-4743, MB-4859, MB-5278, MB-6334, MB-6343, MB-6869, MB-6883, MB-8538, MB-8637, MB-9376, MB-9883

