An import job checks every batch of shift entry records against the integrity rules below before it is loaded; this batch has 10 records. At most one rule is broken, and only in one place. A rule asks for exactly what its sentence says; whatever no listed rule covers is not a violation, however odd it looks.

Record fields: entry_id (text), cost_centre (text), site (text), base_min (whole number), worked_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), project_code (text), allowance_code (text), paid_min (whole number), approved_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), slot (text), corrects (text), overnight (text), row_no (whole number), employee (text), roster (text), extra_min (whole number).

Rules:
R1. `paid_min` is the sum of `base_min` and `extra_min`; any other stored value breaks the rule.
R2. `row_no` rises by exactly 1 from each record to the next one in the batch; the first record may carry any number.
R3. `approved_on` falls at least one day after `worked_on`.
R4. A `employee` that is not a key of the reference list `employees` is not allowed.
R5. A record whose `overnight` is "yes" needs a filled-in `allowance_code`; for other records `allowance_code` is optional.
R6. A `site` is valid only if the reference list `sites` holds it and marks it "active".
R7. Exactly one of `cost_centre` and `project_code` must be filled in: never both, never neither.
R8. Each `corrects` that is given must match the `entry_id` of a preceding record of the batch.
R9. Each `entry_id` appears at most once in the batch; a repeated `entry_id` breaks this rule.

Reference list `employees` with 10 keys: EM-145, EM-317, EM-366, EM-416, EM-686, EM-689, EM-709, EM-886, EM-892, EM-977

Reference list `sites` with 9 entries (key: status): ST-19: active; ST-29: inactive; ST-31: suspended; ST-34: inactive; ST-47: active; ST-76: inactive; ST-80: active; ST-84: active; ST-92: active

