Below are the integrity rules for support ticket records and one batch of 24 records to be checked against them. The batch breaks one of the rules at most, and then at a single spot. Only the listed rules are checked and each is read literally: data that looks odd but is covered by no rule does not count.

Fields per record: ticket_id (text), contact_box (text), case_no (text), billed_min (whole number), team (text), closed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), escalated (text), account (text), reopens (text), opened_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), remote_min (whole number), onsite_min (whole number), manager (text), channel (text), queue_pos (whole number), contact_ext (text).

Rules to check:
R1. `ticket_id` is the record key: a value may occur only once in the batch.
R2. No two records agree in both `account` and `case_no`; agreeing in just one of the two is fine.
R3. A record whose `escalated` is "yes" needs a filled-in `manager`; for other records `manager` is optional.
R4. No value of `channel` other than "mail", "chat", "walk_in" is accepted.
R5. A filled-in `reopens` names the `ticket_id` of another record of this batch, and that record comes before the one that names it.
R6. A `team` that is not a key of the reference list `teams` is not allowed.
R7. The values of `queue_pos` are consecutive in batch order: no number is skipped or repeated, wherever the count starts.
R8. `contact_box` and `contact_ext` exclude each other, and one of them is always filled in.
R9. In every record, `remote_min` and `onsite_min` add up to exactly `billed_min`.

Reference list `teams` with 14 keys, one per line:
TM-10
TM-19
TM-26
TM-27
