An import job checks every batch of support ticket records against the integrity rules below before it is loaded; this batch has 24 records. Either every rule holds or exactly one is broken, at one spot. Nothing is a violation unless a listed rule, taken literally, forbids it.

Fields per record: ticket_id (text), remote_min (whole number), channel (text), queue_pos (whole number), onsite_min (whole number), contact_ext (text), case_no (text), reply_channel (text), manager (text), product (text), escalated (text), team (text), opened_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), closed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), billed_min (whole number), account (text), contact_box (text).

Rules to check:
R1. Going down the batch, each `queue_pos` is the `queue_pos` of the record before it plus 1 (the starting number is free).
R2. Every value of `product` must be an entry of the reference list `products` that is marked "open".
R3. `ticket_id` is the record key: a value may occur only once in the batch.
R4. `billed_min` must equal `remote_min` plus `onsite_min`.
R5. Each `team` has to be found among the keys of the reference list `teams`.
R6. `contact_ext` and `contact_box` exclude each other, and one of them is always filled in.
R7. `opened_on` lies strictly before `closed_on`: the same day breaks the rule.
R8. `channel` is restricted to this list: "portal", "phone", "fax", "chat".

