An import job checks every batch of course enrolment records against the integrity rules below before it is loaded; this batch has 10 records. The batch breaks one of the rules at most, and then at a single spot. Only the listed rules are checked and each is read literally: data that looks odd but is covered by no rule does not count.

Each record has these fields: enrol_id (text), credits (whole number), level (text), needs_lab (text), transfer_of (text), starts_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), module (text), student (text), invoice_ref (text), list_pos (whole number), prior_level (text), tutor (text), ends_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), course (text), lab_slot (text), grant_id (text).

Integrity rules:
R1. Each `transfer_of` that is given must match the `enrol_id` of a preceding record of the batch.
R2. No record has a `ends_on` that lies before its `starts_on`; the two may coincide.
R3. A combination of `student` and `module` that turns up in two records is not allowed, although each of the two fields may repeat by itself.
R4. `tutor` must be a key of the reference list `tutors` whose status there is "current".
R5. No value of `level` other than "intro", "capstone", "advanced" is accepted.
R6. The reference list `courses` must contain every value of `course`.
R7. `credits` stays within 3 to 10, limits included.
R8. The values of `list_pos` are consecutive in batch order: no number is skipped or repeated, wherever the count starts.

Reference list `tutors` with 8 entries (key: status): TU-20: expired; TU-25: expired; TU-32: draft; TU-33: current; TU-45: current; TU-87: current; TU-89: current; TU-97: current

Reference list `courses` with 9 entries (key: status): CR-283: closed; CR-394: open; CR-543: open; CR-560: open; CR-701: open; CR-808: paused; CR-858: open; CR-920: open; CR-993: closed

The status written beside a key counts only for a rule that names a status. Every other rule that refers to the list accepts all of its keys, whatever their status.

