An import job checks every batch of shift entry records against the integrity rules below before it is loaded; this batch has 16 records. The batch breaks one of the rules at most, and then at a single spot. Only the listed rules are checked and each is read literally: data that looks odd but is covered by no rule does not count.

Each record has these fields: entry_id (text), base_min (whole number), slot (text), cover_role (text), role (text), worked_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), corrects (text), cost_centre (text), paid_min (whole number), row_no (whole number), approved_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), site (text), roster (text), extra_min (whole number), project_code (text).

Integrity rules:
R1. A `site` is valid only if the reference list `sites` holds it and marks it "current".
R2. A combination of `slot` and `roster` that turns up in two records is not allowed, although each of the two fields may repeat by itself.
R3. Accepted values of `role`: "checker", "cleaner", "picker", "driver"; nothing else.
R4. The values of `row_no` are consecutive in batch order: no number is skipped or repeated, wherever the count starts.
R5. Each `entry_id` appears at most once in the batch; a repeated `entry_id` breaks this rule.
R6. `worked_on` lies strictly before `approved_on`: the same day breaks the rule.
R7. `paid_min` must equal `base_min` plus `extra_min`.

