Below are the integrity rules for order line records and one batch of 16 records to be checked against them. At most one rule is broken, and only in one place. A rule asks for exactly what its sentence says; whatever no listed rule covers is not a violation, however odd it looks.

Each record has these fields: line_id (text), ship_mode (text), gift_note (text), order_no (text), shipped_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), sku (text), customer (text), tier (text), discount_pct (whole number), return_mode (text), line_no (whole number), quantity (whole number), replaces (text), placed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), gift (text), line_total (whole number), unit_price (whole number).

Rules to check:
R1. Whenever `gift` is "yes", `gift_note` must be filled in.
R2. `customer` must be one of the keys of the reference list `customers`.
R3. `ship_mode` must be one of the following: "freight", "rail", "standard", "express".
R4. The values of `line_no` are consecutive in batch order: no number is skipped or repeated, wherever the count starts.
R5. `order_no` and `sku` together identify a record: two records with the same `order_no` must differ in `sku`.
R6. `discount_pct` must be from 0 to 10 when `tier` is "retail" and from 5 to 30 when `tier` is "trade"; the limits are included.
R7. Each `replaces` that is given must match the `line_id` of a preceding record of the batch.

