A loader validates parcel records batch by batch; the rules it applies and the next batch (16 records) are shown below. At most one rule is broken, and only in one place. A rule asks for exactly what its sentence says; whatever no listed rule covers is not a violation, however odd it looks.

Each record has these fields: parcel_id (text), delivered_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), gross_weight (whole number), outer_packaging (text), sent_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), packaging (text), piece_weight (whole number), manifest (text), declared_value (whole number), scan_no (whole number), depot (text), signature (text), receiver (text), pieces (whole number), forwarded_from (text), route (text), bag (text).

Integrity rules:
R1. Every value of `route` must be an entry of the reference list `routes` that is marked "current".
R2. The pair (`manifest`, `bag`) may occur only once in the batch; either field on its own may repeat.
R3. Each `depot` has to be found among the keys of the reference list `depots`.
R4. `gross_weight` must equal `pieces` multiplied by `piece_weight`.
R5. No value of `packaging` other than "sack", "pallet", "envelope", "tube" is accepted.
R6. When `forwarded_from` is filled in, it must be the `parcel_id` of a record that stands earlier in the batch.
R7. A record whose `signature` is "yes" needs a filled-in `receiver`; for other records `receiver` is optional.
R8. `declared_value` is never smaller than 50 and never larger than 2000.

