A loader validates sensor reading records batch by batch; the rules it applies and the next batch (10 records) are shown below. No batch breaks more than one rule, and a broken rule is broken once. Judge the batch by the listed rules alone, reading each one literally; anything they do not mention is acceptable.

Record fields: reading_id (text), gateway (text), frame_no (whole number), cycle (text), temp_c (whole number), script_id (text), zone (text), operator (text), mode (text), sensor (text), prior_mode (text), probe (text).

Rules:
R1. Each `gateway` has to appear in the reference list `gateways` with the status "open"; an entry with any other status does not count.
R2. For a record with `zone` = "ambient", `temp_c` lies between 7 and 38; for a record with `zone` = "chilled", between 2 and 10 (limits included in both cases).
R3. `script_id` and `operator` exclude each other, and one of them is always filled in.
R4. Each `sensor` has to be found among the keys of the reference list `sensors`.
R5. Each `reading_id` appears at most once in the batch; a repeated `reading_id` breaks this rule.
R6. No value of `mode` other than "test", "auto", "sleep" is accepted.

Reference list `gateways` with 8 entries (key: status): GW-22: paused; GW-33: open; GW-59: open; GW-60: open; GW-68: open; GW-71: closed; GW-79: open; GW-95: closed

Reference list `sensors` with 8 keys: SN-101, SN-134, SN-160, SN-169, SN-320, SN-769, SN-783, SN-796

The status written beside a key counts only for a rule that names a status. Every other rule that refers to the list accepts all of its keys, whatever their status.

