Below are the integrity rules for sensor reading records and one batch of 16 records to be checked against them. Either every rule holds or exactly one is broken, at one spot. Nothing is a violation unless a listed rule, taken literally, forbids it.

Each record has these fields: reading_id (text), script_id (text), taken_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), operator (text), probe (text), prior_mode (text), cycle (text), temp_c (whole number), valid_samples (whole number), zone (text), recheck_of (text), mode (text), uploaded_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), void_samples (whole number), all_samples (whole number), sensor (text), frame_no (whole number).

Rules:
R1. Going down the batch, each `frame_no` is the `frame_no` of the record before it plus 1 (the starting number is free).
R2. Accepted values of `mode`: "sleep", "manual", "test", "idle"; nothing else.
R3. `temp_c` must be from 5 to 35 when `zone` is "ambient" and from 0 to 8 when `zone` is "chilled"; the limits are included.
R4. `all_samples` is the sum of `valid_samples` and `void_samples`; any other stored value breaks the rule.
R5. A combination of `probe` and `cycle` that turns up in two records is not allowed, although each of the two fields may repeat by itself.
R6. Within the batch, all values of `reading_id` differ from one another.
R7. A `sensor` that is not a key of the reference list `sensors` is not allowed.
R8. A filled-in `recheck_of` names the `reading_id` of another record of this batch, and that record comes before the one that names it.

Reference list `sensors` with 12 entries (key: status): SN-130: active; SN-185: active; SN-257: active; SN-342: suspended; SN-401: inactive; SN-450: active; SN-482: active; SN-540: suspended; SN-547: active; SN-561: active; SN-565: active; SN-580: suspended

A listed key is a listed key, whatever its status: only a rule that names a status asks for it, and any other rule that refers to the list accepts all of its keys.

