Below are the integrity rules for library loan records and one batch of 16 records to be checked against them. Either every rule holds or exactly one is broken, at one spot. Nothing is a violation unless a listed rule, taken literally, forbids it.

Record fields: loan_id (text), reserve_code (text), condition_out (text), branch (text), member (text), renewals (whole number), item_type (text), slip_no (whole number), deposit (whole number), desk (text), card (text), reserved (text), renewal_of (text), due_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), copy (text), borrowed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), kiosk (text).

Integrity rules:
R1. `branch` must be a key of the reference list `branches` whose status there is "current".
R2. `renewals` is never smaller than 0 and never larger than 3.
R3. Each `member` has to be found among the keys of the reference list `members`.
R4. For a record with `item_type` = "book", `deposit` lies between 6 and 30; for a record with `item_type` = "media", between 2 and 9 (limits included in both cases).
R5. A filled-in `renewal_of` names the `loan_id` of another record of this batch, and that record comes before the one that names it.
R6. A combination of `copy` and `card` that turns up in two records is not allowed, although each of the two fields may repeat by itself.
R7. Whenever `reserved` is "yes", `reserve_code` must be filled in.
R8. `due_on` falls at least one day after `borrowed_on`.
R9. `condition_out` is restricted to this list: "worn", "repaired", "new".

Reference list `branches` with 11 entries (key: status): BR-12: current; BR-26: current; BR-31: expired; BR-58: current; BR-72: draft; BR-75: expired; BR-78: expired; BR-81: current; BR-84: current; BR-87: current; BR-91: current

