24 shift entry records were delivered as one batch and are validated against the numbered rules that follow. Either every rule holds or exactly one is broken, at one spot. Nothing is a violation unless a listed rule, taken literally, forbids it.

Each record has these fields: entry_id (text), row_no (whole number), worked_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), grade (text), extra_min (whole number), approved_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), base_min (whole number), allowance_code (text), cost_centre (text), project_code (text), overnight (text), paid_min (whole number), hourly_rate (whole number), corrects (text).

Integrity rules:
R1. The values of `row_no` are consecutive in batch order: no number is skipped or repeated, wherever the count starts.
R2. `paid_min` is the sum of `base_min` and `extra_min`; any other stored value breaks the rule.
R3. Within the batch, all values of `entry_id` differ from one another.
R4. A record whose `overnight` is "yes" needs a filled-in `allowance_code`; for other records `allowance_code` is optional.
R5. For a record with `grade` = "senior", `hourly_rate` lies between 19 and 48; for a record with `grade` = "junior", between 11 and 22 (limits included in both cases).
R6. `corrects` may be empty; otherwise it holds a `entry_id` found in one of the records above it.

