An import job checks every batch of course enrolment records against the integrity rules below before it is loaded; this batch has 24 records. Either every rule holds or exactly one is broken, at one spot. Nothing is a violation unless a listed rule, taken literally, forbids it.

Record fields: enrol_id (text), starts_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), list_pos (whole number), invoice_ref (text), materials (whole number), lab_slot (text), level (text), ends_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), credits (whole number), tutor (text), student (text), amount_due (whole number), grant_id (text), transfer_of (text), needs_lab (text), module (text), tuition (whole number).

Rules to check:
R1. `lab_slot` may be empty only if `needs_lab` is not "yes".
R2. `credits` is never smaller than 1 and never larger than 8.
R3. No value of `level` other than "intro", "seminar", "core", "bridge" is accepted.
R4. A filled-in `transfer_of` names the `enrol_id` of another record of this batch, and that record comes before the one that names it.
R5. `starts_on` precedes `ends_on`, and the two never coincide.
R6. Each record fills in either `grant_id` or `invoice_ref`, but not both and not neither.
R7. `amount_due` is the sum of `tuition` and `materials`; any other stored value breaks the rule.
R8. A `tutor` is valid only if the reference list `tutors` holds it and marks it "current".

Reference list `tutors` with 14 entries, one per line (key: status):
TU-11: current
