Below are the integrity rules for support ticket records and one batch of 24 records to be checked against them. No batch breaks more than one rule, and a broken rule is broken once. Judge the batch by the listed rules alone, reading each one literally; anything they do not mention is acceptable.

Each record has these fields: ticket_id (text), onsite_min (whole number), reopens (text), account (text), manager (text), remote_min (whole number), escalated (text), billed_min (whole number), closed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), case_no (text), severity (whole number), queue_pos (whole number), opened_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), channel (text).

Rules:
R1. Going down the batch, each `queue_pos` is the `queue_pos` of the record before it plus 1 (the starting number is free).
R2. `severity` stays within 1 to 6, limits included.
R3. Within the batch, all values of `ticket_id` differ from one another.
R4. `manager` may be empty only if `escalated` is not "yes".
R5. `reopens` may be empty; otherwise it holds a `ticket_id` found in one of the records above it.
R6. Accepted values of `channel`: "walk_in", "portal", "chat"; nothing else.
R7. The stored `billed_min` has to match `remote_min` + `onsite_min`.

