An import job checks every batch of support ticket records against the integrity rules below before it is loaded; this batch has 16 records. Either every rule holds or exactly one is broken, at one spot. Nothing is a violation unless a listed rule, taken literally, forbids it.

Record fields: ticket_id (text), manager (text), queue_pos (whole number), channel (text), reply_channel (text), contact_box (text), account (text), billed_min (whole number), remote_min (whole number), escalated (text), team (text), onsite_min (whole number), contact_ext (text), reopens (text), case_no (text).

Rules:
R1. `billed_min` must equal `remote_min` plus `onsite_min`.
R2. The values of `queue_pos` are consecutive in batch order: no number is skipped or repeated, wherever the count starts.
R3. Each record fills in either `contact_box` or `contact_ext`, but not both and not neither.
R4. Each `reopens` that is given must match the `ticket_id` of a preceding record of the batch.
R5. `ticket_id` is the record key: a value may occur only once in the batch.
R6. `channel` must be one of the following: "fax", "portal", "chat".
R7. `team` must be one of the keys of the reference list `teams`.

Reference list `teams` with 10 entries, one per line (key: status):
TM-29: approved
TM-33: revoked
TM-36: revoked
TM-46: approved
TM-49: pending
TM-57: revoked
TM-69: approved
TM-70: approved
TM-90: approved
TM-96: approved

Note on statuses: a plain look-up rule (one that names no status) accepts any key of the list, with any status; a status is required only by a rule that names it.

