An import job checks every batch of invoice line records against the integrity rules below before it is loaded; this batch has 16 records. At most one rule is broken, and only in one place. A rule asks for exactly what its sentence says; whatever no listed rule covers is not a violation, however odd it looks.

Record fields: line_ref (text), item_code (text), invoice_no (text), due_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), net (whole number), contract_no (text), dispute_code (text), cost_unit (text), currency (text), po_number (text), disputed (text), gross (whole number), tax (whole number), page_line (whole number), issued_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD).

Rules:
R1. The pair (`item_code`, `invoice_no`) may occur only once in the batch; either field on its own may repeat.
R2. The stored `gross` has to match `net` + `tax`.
R3. `cost_unit` must be a key of the reference list `cost_units` whose status there is "open".
R4. `dispute_code` may be empty only if `disputed` is not "yes".
R5. `line_ref` is the record key: a value may occur only once in the batch.
R6. Accepted values of `currency`: "USD", "NOK", "GBP"; nothing else.
R7. Going down the batch, each `page_line` is the `page_line` of the record before it plus 1 (the starting number is free).

Reference list `cost_units` with 10 entries (key: status): KU-13: open; KU-18: paused; KU-20: open; KU-23: open; KU-27: open; KU-28: open; KU-47: paused; KU-51: open; KU-70: paused; KU-81: open

