A loader validates order line records batch by batch; the rules it applies and the next batch (10 records) are shown below. At most one rule is broken, and only in one place. A rule asks for exactly what its sentence says; whatever no listed rule covers is not a violation, however odd it looks.

Record fields: line_id (text), unit_price (whole number), quantity (whole number), gift (text), shipped_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), warehouse (text), customer (text), line_no (whole number), delivery_zone (text), placed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), gift_note (text), line_total (whole number), pickup_point (text).

Integrity rules:
R1. In every record, `quantity` times `unit_price` gives exactly `line_total`.
R2. `customer` must be one of the keys of the reference list `customers`.
R3. A `warehouse` is valid only if the reference list `warehouses` holds it and marks it "current".
R4. A record whose `gift` is "yes" needs a filled-in `gift_note`; for other records `gift_note` is optional.
R5. The values of `line_no` are consecutive in batch order: no number is skipped or repeated, wherever the count starts.
R6. Within the batch, all values of `line_id` differ from one another.

Reference list `customers` with 10 entries (key: status): CU-117: pending; CU-124: approved; CU-193: approved; CU-255: approved; CU-442: approved; CU-482: approved; CU-487: pending; CU-577: approved; CU-866: approved; CU-955: approved

Reference list `warehouses` with 8 entries (key: status): WH-12: draft; WH-53: current; WH-57: current; WH-67: current; WH-75: expired; WH-88: current; WH-89: expired; WH-93: current

Note on statuses: a plain look-up rule (one that names no status) accepts any key of the list, with any status; a status is required only by a rule that names it.

