An import job checks every batch of sensor reading records against the integrity rules below before it is loaded; this batch has 24 records. The batch breaks one of the rules at most, and then at a single spot. Only the listed rules are checked and each is read literally: data that looks odd but is covered by no rule does not count.

Each record has these fields: reading_id (text), script_id (text), alarm (text), alarm_code (text), uploaded_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), sensor (text), probe (text), taken_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), zone (text), temp_c (whole number), prior_mode (text), recheck_of (text), cycle (text), operator (text), frame_no (whole number), mode (text).

Integrity rules:
R1. If `zone` is "ambient", `temp_c` is at least 6 and at most 32; if `zone` is "chilled", at least 1 and at most 9.
R2. The values of `frame_no` are consecutive in batch order: no number is skipped or repeated, wherever the count starts.
R3. Whenever `alarm` is "yes", `alarm_code` must be filled in.
R4. No two records agree in both `cycle` and `probe`; agreeing in just one of the two is fine.
R5. Accepted values of `mode`: "calibration", "manual", "auto", "sleep"; nothing else.
R6. A filled-in `recheck_of` names the `reading_id` of another record of this batch, and that record comes before the one that names it.
R7. `taken_on` precedes `uploaded_on`, and the two never coincide.
R8. `sensor` must be one of the keys of the reference list `sensors`.

Reference list `sensors` with 15 entries (key: status): SN-178: current; SN-202: draft; SN-315: current; SN-356: current; SN-434: current; SN-460: draft; SN-557: draft; SN-629: draft; SN-757: current; SN-764: current; SN-806: current; SN-814: current; SN-822: current; SN-828: current; SN-857: expired

A listed key is a listed key, whatever its status: only a rule that names a status asks for it, and any other rule that refers to the list accepts all of its keys.

