An import job checks every batch of support ticket records against the integrity rules below before it is loaded; this batch has 10 records. The batch breaks one of the rules at most, and then at a single spot. Only the listed rules are checked and each is read literally: data that looks odd but is covered by no rule does not count.

Record fields: ticket_id (text), closed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), case_no (text), reopens (text), severity (whole number), contact_ext (text), remote_min (whole number), channel (text), product (text), manager (text), opened_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), queue_pos (whole number), onsite_min (whole number), contact_box (text), account (text), escalated (text), billed_min (whole number).

Integrity rules:
R1. Within the batch, all values of `ticket_id` differ from one another.
R2. Each `reopens` that is given must match the `ticket_id` of a preceding record of the batch.
R3. `product` must be a key of the reference list `products` whose status there is "active".
R4. The stored `billed_min` has to match `remote_min` + `onsite_min`.
R5. A combination of `account` and `case_no` that turns up in two records is not allowed, although each of the two fields may repeat by itself.
R6. Each record fills in either `contact_box` or `contact_ext`, but not both and not neither.
R7. `closed_on` falls at least one day after `opened_on`.
R8. `severity` stays within 1 to 5, limits included.
R9. No value of `channel` other than "phone", "chat", "portal" is accepted.

Reference list `products` with 8 entries (key: status): PR-377: suspended; PR-392: active; PR-443: suspended; PR-463: active; PR-563: active; PR-648: active; PR-784: inactive; PR-821: active

The status written beside a key counts only for a rule that names a status. Every other rule that refers to the list accepts all of its keys, whatever their status.

