A loader validates parcel records batch by batch; the rules it applies and the next batch (10 records) are shown below. The batch breaks one of the rules at most, and then at a single spot. Only the listed rules are checked and each is read literally: data that looks odd but is covered by no rule does not count.

Record fields: parcel_id (text), route (text), piece_weight (whole number), bag (text), locker_id (text), gross_weight (whole number), scan_no (whole number), forwarded_from (text), door_code (text), service (text), length_cm (whole number), packaging (text), manifest (text), sent_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), pieces (whole number), delivered_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), declared_value (whole number).

Rules:
R1. Accepted values of `packaging`: "crate", "pallet", "tube", "sack"; nothing else.
R2. If `service` is "letter", `length_cm` is at least 10 and at most 45; if `service` is "freight", at least 40 and at most 180.
R3. Every value of `route` must be an entry of the reference list `routes` that is marked "approved".
R4. A combination of `bag` and `manifest` that turns up in two records is not allowed, although each of the two fields may repeat by itself.
R5. No record has a `delivered_on` that lies before its `sent_on`; the two may coincide.
R6. In every record, `pieces` times `piece_weight` gives exactly `gross_weight`.
R7. No two records of the batch may carry the same `parcel_id`.
R8. `declared_value` must be a whole number from 10 to 2000; 10 and 2000 themselves are allowed.
R9. `forwarded_from` may be empty; otherwise it holds a `parcel_id` found in one of the records above it.

Reference list `routes` with 8 entries (key: status): RT-24: approved; RT-36: approved; RT-39: approved; RT-49: approved; RT-56: revoked; RT-59: approved; RT-86: pending; RT-87: approved

Note on statuses: a plain look-up rule (one that names no status) accepts any key of the list, with any status; a status is required only by a rule that names it.

