An import job checks every batch of order line records against the integrity rules below before it is loaded; this batch has 24 records. The batch breaks one of the rules at most, and then at a single spot. Only the listed rules are checked and each is read literally: data that looks odd but is covered by no rule does not count.

Record fields: line_id (text), return_mode (text), warehouse (text), replaces (text), tier (text), gift_note (text), discount_pct (whole number), placed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), ship_mode (text), line_no (whole number), gift (text), delivery_zone (text), pickup_point (text), shipped_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD).

Rules:
R1. `gift_note` may be empty only if `gift` is not "yes".
R2. Within the batch, all values of `line_id` differ from one another.
R3. Accepted values of `ship_mode`: "freight", "economy", "rail", "standard"; nothing else.
R4. A `warehouse` is valid only if the reference list `warehouses` holds it and marks it "current".
R5. Going down the batch, each `line_no` is the `line_no` of the record before it plus 1 (the starting number is free).
R6. For a record with `tier` = "trade", `discount_pct` lies between 5 and 30; for a record with `tier` = "retail", between 0 and 10 (limits included in both cases).
R7. `replaces` may be empty; otherwise it holds a `line_id` found in one of the records above it.

