A loader validates library loan records batch by batch; the rules it applies and the next batch (16 records) are shown below. The batch breaks one of the rules at most, and then at a single spot. Only the listed rules are checked and each is read literally: data that looks odd but is covered by no rule does not count.

Each record has these fields: loan_id (text), slip_no (whole number), reserve_code (text), item_type (text), desk (text), copy (text), borrowed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), renewal_of (text), branch (text), kiosk (text), deposit (whole number), due_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), reserved (text), card (text), member (text).

Integrity rules:
R1. Of the two fields `kiosk` and `desk`, one is filled in and the other is empty, in every record.
R2. A record whose `reserved` is "yes" needs a filled-in `reserve_code`; for other records `reserve_code` is optional.
R3. No two records of the batch may carry the same `loan_id`.
R4. A `member` that is not a key of the reference list `members` is not allowed.
R5. Each `renewal_of` that is given must match the `loan_id` of a preceding record of the batch.
R6. If `item_type` is "media", `deposit` is at least 2 and at most 9; if `item_type` is "book", at least 6 and at most 30.
R7. `borrowed_on` lies strictly before `due_on`: the same day breaks the rule.
R8. A `branch` is valid only if the reference list `branches` holds it and marks it "open".

