An import job checks every batch of invoice line records against the integrity rules below before it is loaded; this batch has 24 records. No batch breaks more than one rule, and a broken rule is broken once. Judge the batch by the listed rules alone, reading each one literally; anything they do not mention is acceptable.

Each record has these fields: line_ref (text), handling_fee (whole number), tax (whole number), due_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), credit_for (text), dispute_code (text), disputed (text), gross (whole number), invoice_no (text), terms (text), item_code (text), cost_unit (text), net (whole number), settle_currency (text), currency (text), issued_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD).

Rules to check:
R1. No record has a `due_on` on or before its `issued_on`.
R2. Whenever `disputed` is "yes", `dispute_code` must be filled in.
R3. For a record with `terms` = "credit", `handling_fee` lies between 7 and 45; for a record with `terms` = "prepaid", between 0 and 10 (limits included in both cases).
R4. Accepted values of `currency`: "CHF", "USD", "GBP"; nothing else.
R5. The stored `gross` has to match `net` + `tax`.
R6. `cost_unit` must be a key of the reference list `cost_units` whose status there is "active".

Reference list `cost_units` with 12 entries (key: status): KU-17: inactive; KU-33: active; KU-36: suspended; KU-45: active; KU-46: active; KU-60: active; KU-61: active; KU-83: suspended; KU-85: active; KU-86: suspended; KU-87: active; KU-89: active

Note on statuses: a plain look-up rule (one that names no status) accepts any key of the list, with any status; a status is required only by a rule that names it.

