16 library loan records were delivered as one batch and are validated against the numbered rules that follow. At most one rule is broken, and only in one place. A rule asks for exactly what its sentence says; whatever no listed rule covers is not a violation, however odd it looks.

Record fields: loan_id (text), daily_fee (whole number), kiosk (text), card (text), desk (text), renewal_of (text), renewals (whole number), reserve_code (text), fine (whole number), days_late (whole number), condition_out (text), copy (text), slip_no (whole number), deposit (whole number), branch (text), item_type (text), reserved (text).

Rules to check:
R1. For a record with `item_type` = "media", `deposit` lies between 3 and 10; for a record with `item_type` = "book", between 7 and 28 (limits included in both cases).
R2. A filled-in `renewal_of` names the `loan_id` of another record of this batch, and that record comes before the one that names it.
R3. Each `branch` has to appear in the reference list `branches` with the status "active"; an entry with any other status does not count.
R4. A record whose `reserved` is "yes" needs a filled-in `reserve_code`; for other records `reserve_code` is optional.
R5. Of the two fields `desk` and `kiosk`, one is filled in and the other is empty, in every record.
R6. `fine` is the product of `days_late` and `daily_fee`; any other stored value breaks the rule.
R7. `condition_out` is restricted to this list: "marked", "worn", "repaired".
R8. `renewals` must be a whole number from 0 to 4; 0 and 4 themselves are allowed.
R9. No two records of the batch may carry the same `loan_id`.

Reference list `branches` with 10 entries (key: status): BR-10: active; BR-25: suspended; BR-32: suspended; BR-35: active; BR-42: active; BR-68: suspended; BR-76: active; BR-82: active; BR-85: inactive; BR-92: active

A listed key is a listed key, whatever its status: only a rule that names a status asks for it, and any other rule that refers to the list accepts all of its keys.

