16 sensor reading records were delivered as one batch and are validated against the numbered rules that follow. At most one rule is broken, and only in one place. A rule asks for exactly what its sentence says; whatever no listed rule covers is not a violation, however odd it looks.

Fields per record: reading_id (text), frame_no (whole number), all_samples (whole number), operator (text), probe (text), sensor (text), taken_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), void_samples (whole number), recheck_of (text), gateway (text), uploaded_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), alarm (text), script_id (text), alarm_code (text), valid_samples (whole number), cycle (text).

Rules:
R1. `gateway` must be a key of the reference list `gateways` whose status there is "open".
R2. A combination of `cycle` and `probe` that turns up in two records is not allowed, although each of the two fields may repeat by itself.
R3. `frame_no` rises by exactly 1 from each record to the next one in the batch; the first record may carry any number.
R4. `all_samples` must equal `valid_samples` plus `void_samples`.
R5. A `sensor` that is not a key of the reference list `sensors` is not allowed.
R6. A record whose `alarm` is "yes" needs a filled-in `alarm_code`; for other records `alarm_code` is optional.
R7. No two records of the batch may carry the same `reading_id`.
R8. Each record fills in either `operator` or `script_id`, but not both and not neither.

