An import job checks every batch of invoice line records against the integrity rules below before it is loaded; this batch has 24 records. Either every rule holds or exactly one is broken, at one spot. Nothing is a violation unless a listed rule, taken literally, forbids it.

Fields per record: line_ref (text), due_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), dispute_code (text), invoice_no (text), item_code (text), credit_for (text), terms (text), handling_fee (whole number), issued_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), vendor (text), page_line (whole number), cost_unit (text), tax_rate (whole number), po_number (text), disputed (text), contract_no (text).

Rules:
R1. The allowed span of `handling_fee` depends on `terms`: 5 to 60 for "credit", 0 to 7 for "prepaid", limits included.
R2. `contract_no` and `po_number` exclude each other, and one of them is always filled in.
R3. No two records of the batch may carry the same `line_ref`.
R4. No two records agree in both `invoice_no` and `item_code`; agreeing in just one of the two is fine.
R5. `tax_rate` stays within 0 to 21, limits included.
R6. Every value of `cost_unit` must be an entry of the reference list `cost_units` that is marked "open".
R7. `issued_on` is never after `due_on`; both on the same day is acceptable.
R8. Each `credit_for` that is given must match the `line_ref` of a preceding record of the batch.
R9. The reference list `vendors` must contain every value of `vendor`.

