Below are the integrity rules for invoice line records and one batch of 10 records to be checked against them. Either every rule holds or exactly one is broken, at one spot. Nothing is a violation unless a listed rule, taken literally, forbids it.

Fields per record: line_ref (text), contract_no (text), handling_fee (whole number), issued_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), disputed (text), gross (whole number), invoice_no (text), credit_for (text), terms (text), due_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), tax (whole number), item_code (text), currency (text), net (whole number), po_number (text), page_line (whole number), dispute_code (text).

Rules to check:
R1. Whenever `disputed` is "yes", `dispute_code` must be filled in.
R2. Each `line_ref` appears at most once in the batch; a repeated `line_ref` breaks this rule.
R3. Each `credit_for` that is given must match the `line_ref` of a preceding record of the batch.
R4. `gross` is the sum of `net` and `tax`; any other stored value breaks the rule.
R5. `page_line` rises by exactly 1 from each record to the next one in the batch; the first record may carry any number.
R6. `issued_on` is never after `due_on`; both on the same day is acceptable.
R7. `currency` must be one of the following: "SEK", "EUR", "NOK".
R8. For a record with `terms` = "prepaid", `handling_fee` lies between 0 and 7; for a record with `terms` = "credit", between 5 and 60 (limits included in both cases).
R9. Of the two fields `contract_no` and `po_number`, one is filled in and the other is empty, in every record.

