A loader validates sensor reading records batch by batch; the rules it applies and the next batch (24 records) are shown below. No batch breaks more than one rule, and a broken rule is broken once. Judge the batch by the listed rules alone, reading each one literally; anything they do not mention is acceptable.

Record fields: reading_id (text), temp_c (whole number), operator (text), cycle (text), frame_no (whole number), alarm_code (text), void_samples (whole number), mode (text), probe (text), all_samples (whole number), sensor (text), script_id (text), alarm (text), gateway (text), zone (text), valid_samples (whole number), recheck_of (text).

Rules:
R1. Exactly one of `script_id` and `operator` must be filled in: never both, never neither.
R2. `all_samples` must equal `valid_samples` plus `void_samples`.
R3. `alarm_code` may be empty only if `alarm` is not "yes".
R4. A combination of `probe` and `cycle` that turns up in two records is not allowed, although each of the two fields may repeat by itself.
R5. For a record with `zone` = "ambient", `temp_c` lies between 6 and 32; for a record with `zone` = "chilled", between 1 and 9 (limits included in both cases).
R6. `mode` must be one of the following: "manual", "calibration", "sleep".
R7. The reference list `sensors` must contain every value of `sensor`.
R8. Each `gateway` has to appear in the reference list `gateways` with the status "current"; an entry with any other status does not count.
R9. No two records of the batch may carry the same `reading_id`.

Reference list `sensors` with 12 entries (key: status): SN-219: current; SN-224: current; SN-334: current; SN-362: draft; SN-372: draft; SN-572: current; SN-630: current; SN-712: expired; SN-769: draft; SN-862: current; SN-898: current; SN-945: current

Reference list `gateways` with 13 entries (key: status): GW-11: draft; GW-16: current; GW-18: current; GW-25: current; GW-28: current; GW-45: current; GW-52: current; GW-68: draft; GW-79: current; GW-81: draft; GW-92: current; GW-95: expired; GW-97: draft

Statuses in a reference list: a rule that only asks for a key of the list accepts every key the list holds, whatever its status; only a rule that names a status requires it.

