Below are the integrity rules for support ticket records and one batch of 10 records to be checked against them. No batch breaks more than one rule, and a broken rule is broken once. Judge the batch by the listed rules alone, reading each one literally; anything they do not mention is acceptable.

Fields per record: ticket_id (text), closed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), contact_ext (text), contact_box (text), onsite_min (whole number), opened_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), manager (text), plan (text), severity (whole number), case_no (text), reopens (text), remote_min (whole number), account (text), escalated (text), billed_min (whole number), response_hours (whole number).

Rules to check:
R1. The allowed span of `response_hours` depends on `plan`: 12 to 96 for "basic", 2 to 30 for "premium", limits included.
R2. Whenever `escalated` is "yes", `manager` must be filled in.
R3. `severity` stays within 1 to 6, limits included.
R4. `billed_min` must equal `remote_min` plus `onsite_min`.
R5. `account` and `case_no` together identify a record: two records with the same `account` must differ in `case_no`.
R6. A filled-in `reopens` names the `ticket_id` of another record of this batch, and that record comes before the one that names it.

